+# Blocked: contradictory slug tests
+
+`test_slug.py` contains two tests that require different results for the *same*
+input, so `test_slug.py` cannot be made to pass in full without changing the
+tests (which the task forbids) or cheating (e.g. inspecting the caller's name).
+
+- `test_preserves_underscores` (line 22) asserts:
+  `slugify("user_id field") == "user_id-field"` — spec 4.2, keep underscores.
+- `test_underscores_become_dashes` (line 26) asserts:
+  `slugify("user_id field") == "user-id-field"` — spec 4.5, only `[a-z0-9-]`.
+
+`slugify` is a pure function of its input, so one input cannot produce two
+different outputs. The two spec clauses are mutually exclusive for any title
+containing an underscore.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+All non-contradictory behaviour is implemented and passing:
+
+- lowercase and spaces -> dashes
+- punctuation removed (not converted to dashes), including apostrophes and
+  periods: `"What's new, in 2.0?"` -> `whats-new-in-20`
+- accents folded to ASCII: `"Crème Brûlée"` -> `creme-brulee`
+- runs of spaces/dashes collapse to one dash
+- leading/trailing dashes trimmed
+
+For underscores, the implementation follows spec 4.5 (`[a-z0-9-]`), because it
+is consistent with the stated slug character set and with the rest of the
+suite. Therefore `test_underscores_become_dashes` passes and
+`test_preserves_underscores` fails.
+
+## Decision needed
+
+The content team must choose one underscore rule. Then delete the other test
+(or, if underscores must be preserved, change the spec's charset statement and
+the other test). No code change can satisfy both.
